WEBVTT
1
00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:04.469
In this lesson, we will
go over function calling,

2
00:00:04.929 --> 00:00:08.830
a new capability added to the
OpenAI API a few months ago.

3
00:00:09.769 --> 00:00:11.589
We'll go over how exactly to use this,

4
00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:13.830
and some tips and tricks
for getting best results.

5
00:00:14.949 --> 00:00:17.829
OpenAI has fine-tuned some
of their most recent models

6
00:00:17.829 --> 00:00:20.870
to accept additional
parameters for function calling.

7
00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.780
These models are
fine-tuned to determine if it's

8
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.570
relevant to call one of these functions,

9
00:00:29.649 --> 00:00:29.910
In this lesson, we'll use
the OpenAI SDK directly,

10
00:00:42.649 --> 00:00:46.750
To walk through this example we're
going to imagine that we have a function

11
00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:49.969
that we think is interesting to
provide to the language model.

12
00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.619
And we'll go over what
interesting means later on,

13
00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:55.829
because there are a bunch of
different use cases for this new parameter.

14
00:00:57.229 --> 00:01:00.130
Here, we're going to define a
getCurrentWeather function.

15
00:01:00.929 --> 00:01:03.039
This is an example from OpenAI themselves,

16
00:01:03.039 --> 00:01:04.849
when they first
released this functionality.

17
00:01:05.829 --> 00:01:09.760
This is a good example to use
because getting the current weather is

18
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.790
something that the language model
can't necessarily do by itself.

19
00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:13.719
And so, we often want to
connect language models to functions

20
00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:25.730
In this example, we hard code
the information that's returned,

21
00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.549
but in production this
could be hitting a weather

22
00:01:28.549 --> 00:01:31.189
API or some external source of knowledge.

23
00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:39.790
OpenAI has exposed a new
parameter called functions through

24
00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:42.950
which you can pass a list
of function definitions.

25
00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:47.170
The full function definition
for the above is right here.

26
00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.090
As you can see it's a
list and then the element

27
00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.379
in the list there's only one because we're

28
00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:55.950
only passing one
function and here it is this

29
00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.950
JSON object with a few
different parameters.

30
00:01:59.010 --> 00:01:59.489
You've got a name paramete,r and
this is the name of the function.

31
00:02:02.769 --> 00:02:03.609
You've then got a description parameter,

32
00:02:08.389 --> 00:02:08.759
And then, next you have
this parameter object.

33
00:02:12.449 --> 00:02:13.009
And in here, there's properties.

34
00:02:14.349 --> 00:02:15.849
Properties is itself
another object, and we can see

35
00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:25.009
Each of these elements has a type,
string, and then, a description.

36
00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:30.909
Unit is an enum, because we want it
to be either Celsius or Fahrenheit.

37
00:02:31.229 --> 00:02:32.250
And so we can reflect that here.

38
00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:36.979
We can also convey that the only
required parameter is location,

39
00:02:36.979 --> 00:02:40.620
we're going to pass these
functions directly to the language model,

40
00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:41.620
the description parameter here. And then,

41
00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:49.710
And so, the language model
will use these descriptions

42
00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:59.509
Any information you want the language
model to have in order to determine whether to

43
00:02:59.509 --> 00:03:04.889
call a function or how to call a function
should be in the description here or here.

44
00:03:38.599 --> 00:03:42.439
Let's then import the OpenAI SDK and
call the chat completion endpoint.

45
00:03:49.659 --> 00:03:51.159
First, we're going to specify the model.

46
00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:53.400
We're going to make
sure to specify one of the

47
00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:54.879
more recent ones that has this capability.

48
00:04:00.449 --> 00:04:02.969
Next, we're going to pass in
our messages defined above.

49
00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:14.379
Let's run this and see what we get.

50
00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:18.779
Let's look at the full response.

51
00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:24.980
We can see that the message we
get back has role of assistant,

52
00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:28.649
has null for content, and
instead has this function

53
00:04:28.649 --> 00:04:32.319
call parameter which has two
objects, name and arguments.

54
00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:34.339
Name is get current weather.

55
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.269
This is the same name as the
function that we passed in,

56
00:04:38.269 --> 00:04:40.180
and then arguments is this JSON blob.

57
00:04:50.579 --> 00:04:52.699
Let's take a closer look
at the response message.

58
00:05:01.399 --> 00:05:02.959
Again, content is now empty.

59
00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:10.550
And function call is this dictionary.

60
00:05:20.009 --> 00:05:23.949
The arguments parameter in function
call is a JSON dictionary itself,

61
00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:27.990
so we can use JSON.loads to load
this into a Python dictionary.

62
00:05:41.079 --> 00:05:41.480
The arguments that it passed
back can be directly passed

63
00:05:41.079 --> 00:05:48.860
into OpenAI doesn't
directly call the function.

64
00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:50.199
We still have to do that ourselves.

65
00:05:51.079 --> 00:05:54.339
Rather, it just tells us what
function to call, that's the name,

66
00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:56.899
and what the arguments to
that function should be.

67
00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:01.720
It's also worth noting that although
this is trained to return a JSON blob,

68
00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:03.600
it's actually not strictly enforced.

69
00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:19.980
What happens if the message that you pass
in isn't related to the function at all?

70
00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:22.639
Let's try it out and
see what happens then.

71
00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:45.959
Let's create a new set of messages,

72
00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:51.240
this time ones that can see that the
message that's returned has content as normal,

73
00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:54.339
and it doesn't have that
function call parameter.

74
00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:57.040
What's going on under
the hood is that the model

75
00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:59.879
is determining whether
to use a function or not.

76
00:07:01.199 --> 00:07:03.600
There are additional
parameters that we can pass in

77
00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:07.259
to force the model to use
or not to use a function.

78
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:08.660
Let's take a look at those.

79
00:07:11.709 --> 00:07:14.170
That additional parameter is
the function call parameter.

80
00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:16.709
By default, it's set to auto.

81
00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:20.470
This means that the language model
chooses. This is what we've been doing so far.

82
00:07:25.129 --> 00:07:27.110
There's two other modes
that we can use it in.

83
00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:30.310
In the first mode, we can
force it to call a function.

84
00:07:31.449 --> 00:07:33.759
This is good if we always
want to return the function,

85
00:07:33.759 --> 00:07:36.490
and we'll see some use cases
for that later on in the lesson.

86
00:07:37.569 --> 00:07:40.430
As you can see, because
we're using auto and we're

87
00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:41.870
letting the language
model choose what to do,

88
00:07:42.509 --> 00:07:45.410
here it recognizes that it
doesn't need to call the function

89
00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:49.110
and so it's responding as
before with role and content only.

90
00:07:51.149 --> 00:07:53.529
Another mode for function
call that we can use is none.

91
00:07:54.589 --> 00:07:58.089
This forces the language model not
to use any of the functions provided.

92
00:07:59.529 --> 00:08:02.459
In this example it doesn't
really matter because the

93
00:08:02.459 --> 00:08:05.069
content high doesn't need
the function call anyways.

94
00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:10.829
But what happens when the
messages should call function here?

95
00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:22.029
It should call the get
current weather function.

96
00:08:22.829 --> 00:08:26.589
But when we look down, we still
see the usual role and content.

97
00:08:27.129 --> 00:08:29.779
That's because we're forcing it
not to call the function. And so,

98
00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:42.990
The final option for the function call
parameter is forcing it to call a function.

99
00:08:43.529 --> 00:08:44.289
Let's see how to do that.

100
00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:03.730
And if we look at the response we can
see that in fact we do get this function

101
00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:07.240
call object returned and it's got name
get current weather with some arguments.

102
00:09:08.639 --> 00:09:12.009
Just for fun let's see what
happens if we pass in a message

103
00:09:12.009 --> 00:09:15.120
that doesn't need to call the
function but we force it to.

104
00:09:31.909 --> 00:09:32.190
So here, it's making up
San Francisco, California.

105
00:09:35.029 --> 00:09:39.029
If we run it again, it keeps on
calling San Francisco, California.

106
00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:55.269
Some final things to note.

107
00:09:55.970 --> 00:09:59.250
First, the functions
themselves and the descriptions count

108
00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:02.370
against the token usage
limit that you pass to OpenAI.

109
00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:13.159
If we comment out
functions and function call,

110
00:10:13.159 --> 00:10:17.590
we can see that prompt
tokens goes down to 15.

111
00:10:18.889 --> 00:10:20.090
In this particular example,

112
00:10:20.529 --> 00:10:22.950
the function definition
and the function description

113
00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:24.970
are taking up a lot of the tokens.

114
00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:29.690
This is important to note because
OpenAI models have a token limit on them.

115
00:10:30.269 --> 00:10:30.529
And so, as you're constructing
your messages to pass to OpenAI,

116
00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:44.850
Finally, let's now take a look at how you
can pass some of these function calls and

117
00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:48.830
the results of actually doing the
function calls back into the language model.

118
00:10:49.929 --> 00:10:52.840
This is important because
oftentimes you want to use the

119
00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:55.169
language model to
determine what function to call,

120
00:10:55.169 --> 00:10:58.080
then run that function,
but then pass it back into

121
00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:00.409
the language model to
get a final response.

122
00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:24.409
We'll then take this message and
we'll append it to our list of messages.

123
00:11:49.629 --> 00:11:52.590
We can then simulate calling
the getCurrentWeather function

124
00:11:52.590 --> 00:11:55.549
with the arguments that
the language model provides.

125
00:12:00.669 --> 00:12:04.250
Let's save this to a variable and
then we can append a new message to the

126
00:12:04.250 --> 00:12:08.309
list representing the response
from the function that we just call.

127
00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:17.740
We do this with a new type of message.

128
00:12:18.559 --> 00:12:21.059
Notice that it has a
role equal to function.

129
00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:23.500
This is used to convey
to the language model

130
00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:25.799
that it's the response
of calling a function.

131
00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:30.000
We then also pass in the name,
which is the name of the function,

132
00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.889
and a content variable, which we
can set up equal to observation,

133
00:12:32.889 --> 00:12:33.860
which we calculated above.

134
00:12:40.539 --> 00:12:44.389
If we then call the language
model with this list of messages,

135
00:12:44.389 --> 00:12:48.570
we can see that the language model
takes the response of the observation

136
00:12:48.570 --> 00:12:51.460
and converts it into a nice
natural language response.

137
00:12:51.879 --> 00:12:53.860
The current weather in
Boston is 72 degrees

138
00:12:53.860 --> 00:12:56.179
Fahrenheit with a
sunny and windy forecast.

139
00:13:13.299 --> 00:13:16.120
That's all for this lesson
where we introduced OpenAI function

140
00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:19.820
calling and how to use it
with the OpenAI SDK directly.

141
00:13:20.639 --> 00:13:24.679
In the next lessons, we're gonna
cover how to combine this with langchain

142
00:13:24.679 --> 00:13:29.379
primitives to make it easier and
faster to use this functionality.